# `operators::measure::stieltjes`

Lebesgue–Stieltjes integration: a continuous part given by a distribution function plus a list of jumps.

## Signature

```cpp
operators::Operator operators::measure::stieltjes(std::string_view variable, const math::Function &cdf, double lower, double upper, pluss::RangeView<double> jump_points = {}, pluss::RangeView<double> jump_sizes = {});
```

## What It Does

A distribution function `F` can increase smoothly and by jumps. `stieltjes("x", cdf, a, b, jump_points, jump_sizes)` is the operator `f ↦ ∫ₐᵇ f F′ dx + Σⱼ f(xⱼ) sⱼ`: integrate `f` against the derivative of the *continuous (jump-free) part* `cdf`, then add `f` at each jump point times the jump size. It is the integral against `dF` for a mixed distribution: a density with atoms, such as an insurance claim that is zero with positive probability and otherwise continuous, or a price that can jump to a barrier.

The continuous part is given as a function, not a density: the operator differentiates it. The jump points and sizes are parallel lists (and may be empty). With no jumps it is the integral against `cdf′`, which [`weighted`](weighted.md) with the derivative as the density also computes; with no continuous part (`cdf` equal to 0) it is [`atomic`](atomic.md).

## Parameters

`std::string_view variable`
: The integration variable.

`const math::Function &cdf`
: The continuous, jump-free part of the distribution function; the integral uses its derivative.

`double lower`
: The lower limit.

`double upper`
: The upper limit.

`pluss::RangeView<double> jump_points`
: Locations `xⱼ` of the jumps.

`pluss::RangeView<double> jump_sizes`
: Sizes `sⱼ` of the jumps, one per point.

## Returns

An `Operator` whose application returns `∫ f dF` as a function of the remaining variables.

## Errors

- Inconsistent jump points and sizes, or `lower >= upper`, are refused.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

A mixed distribution on `[0, 1]`: continuous part `F(x) = x/2` (density ½) plus an atom of mass ½ at `x = 1`. The total mass is 1; the mean is `∫ x·½ dx + 1·½ = 0.25 + 0.5 = 0.75`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <vector>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace measure = pluss::operators::measure;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const math::Function cdf = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x * 0.5; });
  const std::vector<double> points = {1.0}, sizes = {0.5};
  const operators::Operator mix = operators::measure::stieltjes("x", cdf, 0.0, 1.0, pluss::RangeView<double>(points), pluss::RangeView<double>(sizes));
  const math::Function one = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x * 0.0 + 1.0; });
  const math::Function id = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x; });
  out.writeln("mass:", mix(one)().item<double>(), "  mean:", mix(id)().item<double>());
}
```

### Example 2

No jumps: the integral against `F′`. With `F(x) = x²` on `[0, 1]` (density `2x`), `E[x] = 2/3`, the same as `weighted` with density `2x`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace measure = pluss::operators::measure;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const math::Function cdf = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x * x; });
  const math::Function density = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return 2.0 * x; });
  const math::Function id = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x; });
  out.writeln("stieltjes:", operators::measure::stieltjes("x", cdf, 0.0, 1.0)(id)().item<double>());
  out.writeln("weighted: ", operators::measure::weighted("x", density, 0.0, 1.0)(id)().item<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

Only jumps: a zero continuous part and atoms at `1` and `3` of sizes `0.3` and `0.7` reproduce the atomic measure.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <vector>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace measure = pluss::operators::measure;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const math::Function zero = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x * 0.0; });
  const std::vector<double> points = {1.0, 3.0}, sizes = {0.3, 0.7};
  const math::Function square = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x * x; });
  out.writeln("stieltjes:", operators::measure::stieltjes("x", zero, 0.0, 4.0, pluss::RangeView<double>(points), pluss::RangeView<double>(sizes))(square)().item<double>());
  out.writeln("atomic:   ", operators::measure::atomic("x", pluss::RangeView<double>(points), pluss::RangeView<double>(sizes))(square)().item<double>(), "  (0.3 + 6.3)");
}
```

